WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.131
Disciplined deallocation starts with a
simple rule:

2
00:00:03.211 --> 00:00:05.140
free only memory the current code owns.

3
00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.617
A pointer parameter is not automatically
owned.

4
00:00:07.697 --> 00:00:11.263
A borrowed reference, interior pointer,
string literal, stack object,

5
00:00:11.343 --> 00:00:15.318
static object, or object owned by another
component should not be released by the

6
00:00:15.398 --> 00:00:16.333
current function.

7
00:00:16.413 --> 00:00:18.868
Centralized cleanup can reduce duplicate
logic.

8
00:00:18.948 --> 00:00:21.287
When a function acquires several heap
objects,

9
00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:25.517
one cleanup section can make release order
visible and keep error paths consistent.

10
00:00:25.597 --> 00:00:29.339
That pattern is useful only when the
ownership state of each object is clear.

11
00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:31.699
Partial initialization needs safe
teardown.

12
00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:35.871
If an object has several allocated fields
and construction fails partway through,

13
00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:39.751
cleanup should release exactly the fields
that were successfully acquired.

14
00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:44.040
Helper functions can make this easier when
they accept well-defined partial states.

15
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.329
Double-free problems often come from
unclear ownership or duplicate cleanup paths.

16
00:00:48.409 --> 00:00:51.273
The code may release a field in both a
helper and its caller,

17
00:00:51.353 --> 00:00:54.276
or release the same object on two
different error branches.

18
00:00:54.356 --> 00:00:58.331
Use-after-free problems come from
continuing to use aliases after the object's

19
00:00:58.411 --> 00:00:59.171
lifetime ends.

20
00:00:59.251 --> 00:01:03.285
Setting a pointer to NULL after free can
help some local cleanup patterns by making

21
00:01:03.365 --> 00:01:04.885
repeated local cleanup safer.

22
00:01:04.965 --> 00:01:06.426
It is not a complete solution.

23
00:01:06.506 --> 00:01:08.962
Other aliases may still point to the freed
object,

24
00:01:09.042 --> 00:01:11.263
and the real control is lifetime
discipline:

25
00:01:11.343 --> 00:01:14.500
make release points obvious and prevent
later access by design.
